## Problem

I needed one place to send people from freelance marketplaces and LinkedIn. It had to explain what I offer quickly and load fast on a phone. It had to be easy for search engines and AI answer engines to read. And I wanted to add posts and projects without editing code. A small business website needs the same things.

## What it does

- **Loads fast on a phone.** Pages are built ahead of time, so they open quickly. Text doesn't jump around while the page loads.
- **Edit without a developer.** Services, projects, posts and site settings are changed in a simple admin screen, not in code.
- **Easy for Google to read.** Every page has its own title and description, plus details that tell search engines who I am and what I offer. A sitemap helps them find every page.
- **Good-looking shared links.** When someone shares a page on social media, it shows its own preview image.
- **An RSS feed.** Readers can follow new posts in their feed reader.
- **Cheap to run.** There is no database to pay for, and hosting is on a free plan that allows commercial use.

## How it helps your business

- **Updates take a few minutes,** with no developer and no code.
- **More ways to be found,** because search engines and AI answer engines can read each page clearly.
- **Low running costs,** with no database and no paid hosting plan.

## How it's built

- **Static by default.** Every page is generated at build time from content in the repo, so there's no database to run or pay for.
- **A git-based CMS.** Services, projects, posts, and site settings are edited in a Keystatic admin UI. Changes are saved as files in the repo, so content has the same history and review flow as code.
- **Technical SEO.** Per-page titles, descriptions, and canonical URLs; generated social share images; JSON-LD for the person, services, posts, and breadcrumbs; a sitemap built from the content; and an RSS feed.
- **Very little JavaScript.** The only client component is the mobile menu toggle. Fonts load through `next/font`, so text doesn't jump around while the page loads.
- **Cheap hosting.** It runs on Cloudflare Workers through the OpenNext adapter, on a free plan that allows commercial use.

## Stack

Next.js (App Router), TypeScript, Keystatic, Markdoc, Tailwind CSS, and Cloudflare Workers.

## Outcome

A site that I can update in a few minutes and that a client can inspect as a working example of the "fast, SEO-ready Next.js website" service.

Lighthouse scores, measured on a local production build:

- **Mobile:** Performance 96 to 99, Accessibility 100, Best Practices 100, SEO 100
- **Desktop:** 100 in all four categories
